    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-hardware">
      <p class="folio-label">02 / WORK ON THE BENCH</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-hardware">Two probes. One bus. Readings in the cloud.</h2>
      <p>
        The grgaGrow firmware runs on a Waveshare ESP32-S3-RS485-CAN Rev 2.2. It polls a temperature-and-moisture probe
        and a second probe that also measures pH over one RS485 bus, queues the samples and uploads them over Wi-Fi.
      </p>
      <section class="field-timeline" aria-labelledby="field-timeline-title">
        <h3 id="field-timeline-title">From the workbench to the lawn</h3>
        <ol class="milestones" role="list">
          {
            milestones.map(([icon, date, title, text]) => (
              <li>
                <span class="milestone-marker"><Icon name={icon} /></span>
                <span class="milestone-date">{date}</span>
                <h4>{title}</h4>
                <p>{text}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class="next-field-step"><strong>Next step</strong> <span>Calibrate the pH and soil moisture readings.</span></p>
      </section>
      <section id="lawn-probe-service" aria-labelledby="service-story-title">
        <h3 id="service-story-title">Why the TH probe kept its old address</h3>
        <div class="bench-grid">
          <div>
            <p>Both probes arrived at Modbus address 02. On the bench I could read each one separately. To use them together, I needed to give the TH probe its own address.</p>
            <p>I traced the fault to the controller. It passed the 8-byte read requests but dropped the 11-byte address write before it reached the probe.</p>
            <p>After the fix, I changed the TH address to 03 and cycled the power. TH still answered at 03 and THP at 02. I could now read both on the same bus.</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="service-board" data-gallery-scope data-gallery-no-filmstrip>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/bench/service-board-ph.jpg"
              alt="Full grgaGrow app window on the Mac with Board status, readings from both probes and pH on THP 02"
              width="1280"
              height="720"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              data-group="grgagrow-service"
              data-index="0"
              data-title="Full grgaGrow Board on the Mac, 24 November 2026. Both probes were reporting over Wi-Fi."
              tabindex="0"
              role="button"
              aria-label="Enlarge the Probe Service Board screenshot"
            />
            <figcaption>
              PROBE SERVICE / Full grgaGrow app window on the Mac, 24 November 2026. Both probes were reporting over
              Wi-Fi.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <details class="evidence-block">
          <summary>Probe Service technical details</summary>
          <p>
            To work on the probes without risking the installed firmware, I built <strong>Probe Service</strong>: a small
            local web app on the Mac, packaged as a Dock app. It loads a bridge image into the ESP32's RAM through the ROM
            bootloader and talks to the probes through it. Nothing is written to flash; a power cycle returns the board to
            its installed firmware.
          </p>
          <dl class="tool-tabs">
            {
              probeServiceTabs.map(([name, text]) => (
                <div>
                  <>
                    <dt>{name}</dt>
                    <dd>{text}</dd>
                  </>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
          <div class="engineering-note firmware-example" aria-labelledby="bridge-example-title">
            <p class="small-label">SAFETY LIMIT / BRIDGE IMAGE</p>
            <h3 id="bridge-example-title">Register reads and probe address changes.</h3>
            <p>
              The bridge forwards only CRC-valid frames to units 1–247: register reads, and one write to the address
              register with a value from 1 to 247. Everything else is answered with DENY.
            </p>
            <p class="note">bridge-image/main/entry.cpp · allowed (excerpt)</p>
            <details class="code-toggle">
              <summary>Show the C++ excerpt</summary>
              <Code
                code={bridgeExample}
                lang="cpp"
                themes={{ light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' }}
                tabindex="0"
                aria-labelledby="bridge-example-title"
                data-firmware-code="true"
              />
            </details>
          </div>
        </details>
